Let $f(x)=\frac{(x+1)^2}{\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{x^{2}+2x+1}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=x^{\frac{3}{2}}+2x^{\frac{1}{2}}+x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}+x^{-\frac{1}{2}}-\frac{1}{2}x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(1,4\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(1)&=\frac{3}{2}\left(1\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}+\left(1\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}-\frac{1}{2}\left(1\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=\frac{3}{2}+1-\frac{1}{2}\\
&=2
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(2\right)}\\
&=-\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- x^{\frac{3}{2}} - 1, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{3 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}{2}=- \frac{3 \sqrt{x}}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=- \frac{3 \sqrt{(4)}}{2}=-3, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(-3)}=\frac{1}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,-9)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-9)&=\frac{1}{3}(x-(4)), \\y&=\frac{x}{3} - \frac{31}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=\frac{1}{3}\cdot 0+\left(- \frac{31}{3}\right), \\y&=- \frac{31}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(0,- \frac{31}{3}\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 2 x^{\frac{2}{3}}, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{4}{3 x^{\frac{1}{3}}}=- \frac{4}{3 \sqrt[3]{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(1)&=- \frac{4}{3 \sqrt[3]{(1)}}=- \frac{4}{3}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{4}{3}\right)}=\frac{3}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(1,-2)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-2)&=\frac{3}{4}(x-(1)), \\y&=\frac{3 x}{4} - \frac{11}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=\frac{3}{4}x+\left(- \frac{11}{4}\right), \\x&=\frac{11}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{11}{3},0\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=2 + \frac{2}{x}, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{2}{x^{2}}=- \frac{2}{x^{2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(2)&=- \frac{2}{(2)^{2}}=- \frac{1}{2}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)}=2.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(2,3)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(3)&=2(x-(2)), \\y&=2 x - 1.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=2\cdot 0+(-1), \\y&=-1.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(0,-1)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=3 x^{\frac{1}{2}} + 2, \\f'(x)&=\frac{3}{2 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}=\frac{3}{2 \sqrt{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=\frac{3}{2 \sqrt{(4)}}=\frac{3}{4}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)}=- \frac{4}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,8)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(8)&=- \frac{4}{3}(x-(4)), \\y&=- \frac{4 x}{3} + \frac{40}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=- \frac{4}{3}x+\left(\frac{40}{3}\right), \\x&=10.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(10,0)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 2 x^{\frac{1}{4}} + 2, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{1}{2 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}=- \frac{1}{2 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(16)&=- \frac{1}{2 (16)^{\frac{3}{4}}}=- \frac{1}{16}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{1}{16}\right)}=16.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(16,-2)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-2)&=16(x-(16)), \\y&=16 x - 258.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=16\cdot 0+(-258), \\y&=-258.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(0,-258)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 3 x^{\frac{3}{2}} - 2, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{9 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}{2}=- \frac{9 \sqrt{x}}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(1)&=- \frac{9 \sqrt{(1)}}{2}=- \frac{9}{2}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{9}{2}\right)}=\frac{2}{9}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(1,-5)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-5)&=\frac{2}{9}(x-(1)), \\y&=\frac{2 x}{9} - \frac{47}{9}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=\frac{2}{9}x+\left(- \frac{47}{9}\right), \\x&=\frac{47}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{47}{2},0\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 2 x^{\frac{2}{3}} + 2, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{4}{3 x^{\frac{1}{3}}}=- \frac{4}{3 \sqrt[3]{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(8)&=- \frac{4}{3 \sqrt[3]{(8)}}=- \frac{2}{3}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{2}{3}\right)}=\frac{3}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(8,-6)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-6)&=\frac{3}{2}(x-(8)), \\y&=\frac{3 x}{2} - 18.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=\frac{3}{2}\cdot 0+(-18), \\y&=-18.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(0,-18)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=1 + \frac{1}{x}, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{1}{x^{2}}=- \frac{1}{x^{2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(2)&=- \frac{1}{(2)^{2}}=- \frac{1}{4}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{1}{4}\right)}=4.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $\left(2,\frac{3}{2}\right)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)&=4(x-(2)), \\y&=4 x - \frac{13}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=4x+\left(- \frac{13}{2}\right), \\x&=\frac{13}{8}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{13}{8},0\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=2 x^{\frac{1}{2}} + 3, \\f'(x)&=\frac{1}{x^{\frac{1}{2}}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(9)&=\frac{1}{\sqrt{(9)}}=\frac{1}{3}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)}=-3.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(9,9)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(9)&=-3(x-(9)), \\y&=- 3 x + 36.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=-3\cdot 0+(36), \\y&=36.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(0,36)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=2 x^{\frac{1}{4}} + 1, \\f'(x)&=\frac{1}{2 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}=\frac{1}{2 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(16)&=\frac{1}{2 (16)^{\frac{3}{4}}}=\frac{1}{16}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{1}{16}\right)}=-16.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(16,5)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(5)&=-16(x-(16)), \\y&=- 16 x + 261.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=-16x+(261), \\x&=\frac{261}{16}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{261}{16},0\right)$.
